  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 320 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nAn upper level trough will dig south across the west coast\nSunday night into Monday, resulting in a southwest upper level \nflow pattern pattern setting up over the region ahead of the\ntrough early next week. Highs will continue to warm into the \nlower to middle 80s across much of the area on Monday and \nTuesday. Mainly dry conditions will occur across the area on \nMonday with the trough remaining well to the west across the \nwest coast on Monday.\n\nThe upper level trough is then expected to move east across the\nregion mid to late week. The ensemble model members suggest \nthat this system will be stronger with better moisture return \nbringing better widespread chances to the area, currently \n50-80%. There continues to be differences between the model \nmembers on the structural and the exact track and timing of the \nsystem and continue to trend slightly slower. The ensemble \nmodel members also continue to show the potential for the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo off the west coast of Mexico to track\nto the northeast into the Baja. There remains the potential for\nthe remnants getting pulled the into the upper level trough \nacross the southwestern U.S. that will move through the region \nduring the middle to end of the week. If the remnants get \npulled into the trough this would increase the amount of \nmoisture available with the system and lead to several rounds of\nrainfall and could lead to the the potential for some heavy \nrainfall developing across portions of the region but there is \nstill uncertainty on the exact track and where any bands would \nset up at this time.",
